        "pn_1": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "ONE",
            "title": "<strong>Cold-pressed organic plant oils</strong>",
            "body": "<p>The base of every product is cold-pressed organic plant oils, and both of those words are deliberate. Cold pressing avoids the heat and solvents of refining, which means it preserves the bioactive compounds that give a plant oil its character: carotenoids, tocopherols, phytosterols and the phenolics that refined oils tend to lose. The research credits these compounds with antioxidant and skin-conditioning qualities, and there is a practical bonus too, since a higher phenolic content also improves the oil's oxidative stability, so the product stays good for longer.</p><p>We choose these oils for what is in them, not for what they cost. The particular oils change with the skin type, but the reasoning does not.</p>",
            "image_alt": ""
          }
        },
        "pn_2": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "TWO",
            "title": "<strong>The microbiome, and what we do about it</strong>",
            "body": "<p>Most of what a moisturiser can do for your skin's microbiome comes from what it does not do. Every product is built at the slightly acidic pH the skin's resident microbes prefer, and nothing in it is there to strip or sterilise. That has the strongest published evidence behind it, and it is a decision rather than an ingredient.</p><p>Two ingredients lend a more active hand. <a href=\"https://biotaderm.com/blogs/ingredients/inulin\">Inulin</a> is a plant-derived prebiotic, food for the microbes already on your skin, with selective feeding shown in the laboratory but not yet on human skin. A <a href=\"https://biotaderm.com/blogs/ingredients/chlorella-vulgaris-extract\">chlorella extract</a> has supplier evidence, at the level its maker tested, for holding back the more troublesome species while sparing the helpful ones. We say what that evidence is and do not round it up. There is more on the <a href=\"/pages/the-skin-microbiome\">skin microbiome page</a>.</p>",
            "image_alt": ""
          }
        },
        "pn_3": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "THREE",
            "title": "<strong>Atlantic algal extracts</strong>",
            "body": "<p>One ingredient links the range to its home. <a href=\"https://biotaderm.com/blogs/ingredients/bladder-wrack-fucus-vesiculosus-extract\">Bladderwrack</a>, <em>Fucus vesiculosus</em>, is the same Atlantic seaweed species you find on the rocks along the north Devon coast. Ours is a cold infusion in sunflower oil, supplied from the UK, so the connection to the coast is one of kinship and origin. Carried in oil, it is the lipophilic fraction, chiefly fucoxanthin, that comes across, and the research credits fucoxanthin with modest antioxidant activity.</p>",
            "image_alt": "Bladderwrack on wet rock at low tide"
          }
        },
        "b_leaveout": {
          "type": "band",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What we leave out",
            "body": "<p>Good formulation is as much about restraint as addition. We leave out synthetic fragrance entirely, and nothing hides behind a \"parfum\" or \"fragrance\" catch-all. Fragrance ingredients are among the more common triggers for irritation and allergy, which matters most for sensitive skin, so the one aromatic in the range is a single named essential oil at a low level, explained just below. Every ingredient we use is listed individually, most abundant first.</p>",
            "caveat": "",
            "variant": "plain",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "chips": "",
            "mark_1": "",
            "mark_2": "",
